            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the gaps pointed out in the bug report and now present a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\)* is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad |D_c|=c-1 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1.** A sunny line (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) meets any diagonal \\(D_c\\) in **at most one** point.  \n   *Reason:* two distinct points of the same diagonal have slope \\(-1\\).\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (at least two non‑sunny lines).**  \n   Suppose at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is non‑sunny.  \n   Consider the three sets  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   R=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   D=D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   All three sets contain exactly \\(n\\) points and are subsets of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *A sunny line can contain at most one point of each of \\(C,R,D\\)* (otherwise it would be respectively vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   If there is **no** non‑sunny line, the \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of each set, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\), at most \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\) and at most \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\).  \n   Since each set actually has \\(n\\) points, the only way to achieve full coverage would be that every sunny line contains **one** point of each set.  \n   But a line cannot pass through three non‑collinear points; the three points \\((1,1)\\in C\\cap R\\), \\((1,n)\\in C\\), and \\((n,1)\\in R\\) are not collinear, so no single line can contain one point from each of the three sets.  \n   Hence a configuration with zero non‑sunny lines is impossible.\n\n   If there is **exactly one** non‑sunny line, it can be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   *Vertical* (or horizontal) lines can cover at most one point of \\(C\\) (or \\(R\\)) and none of the other two sets, so the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would still have to cover all \\(n\\) points of the other two sets – impossible by the same counting argument as above.  \n   *Slope \\(-1\\)* means the line coincides with the diagonal \\(D\\) and therefore covers all points of \\(D\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must then cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\) and the \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\).  \n   Each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C\\) and at most one point of \\(R\\); consequently \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C\\) and at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(R\\), leaving at least one uncovered point in each set – a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Fixed non‑sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Their union covers all points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the first row, the first column or the outer diagonal \\(D\\).\n\n   *Remaining points*  \n\n   \\[\n   U_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\) the point \\((2,b)\\) belongs to \\(U_n\\).  \n   Define, for \\(b=2,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_b:\\; y-1=(b-1)(x-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_b\\) equals \\(b-1\\), which is a positive integer; hence \\(\\ell_b\\) is sunny (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Each \\(\\ell_b\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and through the interior point \\((2,b)\\).  \n   If \\((a',b')\\in U_n\\) with \\((a',b')\\neq(2,b)\\), then the slope of the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) is \\(\\dfrac{b'-1}{a'-1}\\neq b-1\\) (because \\(a'\\ge2\\) and the fraction is reduced), so \\(\\ell_b\\) contains no other point of \\(U_n\\).  \n   The slopes \\(b-1\\) are distinct, therefore the lines \\(\\ell_b\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_b\\mid b=2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(3+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly \\(n-2\\) are sunny (the \\(\\ell_b\\)’s).  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n   * points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_v\\);\n   * points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_h\\);\n   * points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(\\ell_d\\);\n   * any other point belongs to \\(U_n\\) and is of the form \\((2,b)\\) for some \\(b\\), hence lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_b\\).\n\n   Thus we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n   To obtain **fewer** sunny lines, keep the three non‑sunny lines and retain only the first \\(k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_b\\) (where \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \n   The points that were covered exclusively by the omitted sunny lines are all members of the first row, first column or the out