 at \\((1,1)\\); thus the required covering is impossible.  \n\n   *If there were exactly one non\u2011sunny line*, it can be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   - A vertical (resp. horizontal) line can cover at most one point of \\(C\\) (resp. \\(R\\)) and none of the other two sets, leaving at least one point of each of the remaining two sets uncovered \u2013 contradiction.  \n   - A line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with the diagonal \\(D\\) and covers all its \\(n\\) points, but the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C\\) and at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(R\\); consequently at least one point of each of \\(C,R\\) stays uncovered \u2013 contradiction.  \n\n   Hence at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny, proving (3). \u220e  \n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Fixed non\u2011sunny lines* (three of them, all distinct):\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n   \\ell_d:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Their union covers all points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the first row, the first column or the outer diagonal \\(D_{\\,n+1}\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   U_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) pick the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_c=(c-2,\\,2)\\in U_n .\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed \\(c-2\\ge 1\\) and \\(2\\ge 1\\), and \\((c-2)+2=c\\le n\\).)  \n\n   Through the fixed point \\((1,1)\\) and \\(P_c\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c:\\; y-1=\\frac{2-1}{c-2-1}\\,(x-1)=\\frac{1}{c-3}\\,(x-1),\\qquad 3\\le c\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_c\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{1}{c-3}\\).  \n   Since \\(c\\ge 3\\), this slope is a positive rational number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_c\\) is **sunny**.  \n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   For distinct values \\(c\\neq c'\\) the slopes \\(\\dfrac{1}{c-3}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{1}{c'-3}\\) differ, so the lines \\(\\ell_c\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Coverage of \\(U_n\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in U_n\\). Then \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\). Set \\(c=a+b\\) (so \\(3\\le c\\le n\\)).  \n   The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b=c\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b-1}{a-1}= \\frac{c-a-1}{a-1}= \\frac{c-3}{a-1}\\cdot\\frac{1}{c-3}= \\frac{1}{c-3}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_c\\). Consequently **every** point of \\(U_n\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_c\\) (the one indexed by its diagonal sum \\(c\\)).  \n\n   *Full covering.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_c\\mid 3\\le c\\le k+2\\}, & 0\\le k\\le n-2,\\\\[4pt]\n   \\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_c\\mid 3\\le c\\le n\\}, & k=n-2,\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(3+k\\) distinct lines.  \n   The three fixed lines are non\u2011sunny; the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_c\\) are sunny.  \n   By the discussion above, every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the three fixed lines (if it belongs to \\(C\\), \\(R\\) or \\(D_{\\,n+1}\\)) or on a line \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c=a+b\\) (if it belongs to \\(U_n\\)). Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the covering requirement.\n\n   Therefore for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have produced a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. Hence values \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the explicit construction for each \\(k\\) in that range yields the complete description of admissible numbers of sunny lines. \u220e"
