tions, hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_{t}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}.\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we can choose \\(t=b\\) (which satisfies \\(t\\le n-1\\)) and obtain\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{t}{t+1}a + \\frac{1}{t+1}\n   \\Longleftrightarrow (t+1)b = ta + 1\n   \\Longleftrightarrow tb + b = ta + 1\n   \\Longleftrightarrow t(b-a)=1-b .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b-a\\le 1\\); the only possibilities are \\(b-a=0\\) or \\(b-a=1\\).  \n   *If \\(b=a\\)*, then taking \\(t=1\\) gives the line \\(y=\\frac12 x+\\frac12\\) which passes through \\((a,a)\\).  \n   *If \\(b=a+1\\)*, then taking \\(t=a\\) gives the line \\(y=\\frac{a}{a+1}x+\\frac{1}{a+1}\\) which passes through \\((a,a+1)\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{n}\\), proving that \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n   For any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) we combine the two constructions: take the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) and supplement them with \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines taken from the list used for the \\(k=0\\) case (choosing them so that all lines remain distinct).  \n   Because the sunny lines already cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\), adding extra non\u2011sunny lines does not destroy the covering property. Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nConsequently, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). The converse (that no other values can occur) is trivial because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must find, for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), all integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_{i}\\supseteq S_{n}\\);  \n\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are *sunny*, i.e. their slopes are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_{h}(c): y=c\\qquad (c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\n&\\ell_{v}(1): x=1,\\qquad \\ell_{v}(2): x=2,\\\\\n&\\ell_{d}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct because the constants are distinct.  \nWe verify that every \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le 2\\).  \nWhen \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); when \\(a=2\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=2\\).\n\n*If \\(b=n\\)*, then necessarily \\(a=1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \nThe point \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(1+n=n+1\\), hence lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus the covering condition holds, and all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nWe show that every \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nSince \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n*Case \\(b=a\\).*  \nTake \\(t=1\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2}a+\\frac12 = a\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a= a,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,a)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\).\n\n*Case \\(b=a+1\\).*  \nTake \\(t=a\\) (note that \\(a\\le n-1\\) because \\(a+(a+1)\\le n+1\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{a+1}a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of \\(L_{a}\\) evaluated at \\(x=a\\). Hence \\((a,a+1)\\) lies on \\(L_{a}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered, and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Therefore \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configurations for intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)* or *\\(k=n\\)* we already have constructions.\n\n*Assume \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nTake the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) from the construction of Section\u202f2.  \nAdd to them \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines taken from the list used in Section\u202f1, for instance the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-2\\) together with the vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2\\) and, if needed, the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nBecause the sunny lines already cover the whole set \\(S_{n}\\), the additional non\u2011sunny lines do not affect the covering property. All chosen lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
