t+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\nWe show that every \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n\\).  \n\n*If \\(a=b\\)*, choose \\(t=b\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}a+\\frac{1}{b+1}= \\frac{b}{b+1}b+\\frac{1}{b+1}=b,\n\\]  \n\nso \\((b,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{b}\\).\n\n*If \\(a<b\\)*, set \\(t=a\\) (note that \\(a\\le n-1\\)). Then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{a+1}a+\\frac{1}{a+1}=a+1,\n\\]  \n\nhence \\((a,a+1)\\) lies on \\(L_{a}\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(S_{n}\\) is either of the form \\((m,m)\\) or \\((m,m+1)\\) after a suitable permutation of the coordinates (the triangle \\(S_{n}\\) consists exactly of the points on the two “adjacent” diagonals of the integer lattice). Therefore each point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{L_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{n}\\).\n\nThus the covering condition holds with all \\(n\\) lines sunny, proving that \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)* or *\\(k=n\\)* we already have constructions.\n\n*If \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)*, take the sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) from Section 2 and add to them \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines taken from the list used in Section 1 (for instance the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-2\\) together with the vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2\\) and, if needed, the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\)). All chosen lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the covering property is preserved because the sunny lines already cover the whole set \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n------