 The crucial missing piece is a rigorous construction of a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\). The previously advertised family  \n   \\[\n   L_{t}:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}x+\\frac{1}{t+1}\\qquad (1\\le t\\le n)\n   \\]\n   does **not** have the required covering property; the algebraic verification in the earlier answer was incorrect. A correct all\u2011sunny covering has not yet been produced.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The value \\(k=0\\) is definitely attainable.  \n   - For any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) the existence of a suitable configuration is equivalent to the existence of an all\u2011sunny covering of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   - The problem of constructing such an all\u2011sunny covering remains unresolved in this answer.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S_{n}\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the family of lines \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\nThe problem asks for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\) (no sunny lines)\n\nConsider the following collection of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{0}:=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n*All lines are non\u2011sunny*: the first \\(n-1\\) are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), the last one has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Coverage of \\(S_{n}\\)*:  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\).  \n* If \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we must have \\(b=1\\). In this case \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(n+1=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\), and none of the lines is sunny. Consequently the value  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis admissible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reduction to an all\u2011sunny covering\n\nAssume that we have a family \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(S_{n}\\).  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can construct a required configuration as follows:\n\n* Choose arbitrarily \\(k\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal{S}\\); denote this subset by \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\).  \n* Add to \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) any collection \\(\\mathcal{N}_{k}\\) of \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines (for instance the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\)).  \n\nThe total number of lines is \\(|\\mathcal{S}_{k}|+|\\mathcal{N}_{k}|=k+(n-k)=n\\).  \nExactly the lines of \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\) are sunny, so the number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal{S}_{k}\\subseteq\\mathcal{S}\\) and \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) already covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\), the enlarged family also covers \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nHence **the existence of a configuration for every \\(k\\) is equivalent to the existence of an all\u2011sunny covering \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) of size \\(n\\).**\n\n---\n\n### 4.  The missing all\u2011sunny covering\n\nThe previously advertised family  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}x+\\frac{1}{t+1}\\qquad (1\\le t\\le n)\n\\]\n\nwas claimed to cover \\(S_{n}\\). A direct algebraic check shows that for a generic point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) the equality  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{a^{2}+ab-a+1}{a+b}\n\\]\n\ndoes **not** hold in general; consequently the covering property fails.  \nAt present I have not succeeded