.2),  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (3.5).\n\nThe total number is \\((n-k-1)+1+k=n\\). Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required. All points of \\(T_n\\) are covered: those with \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) or \\(a=n-k\\) lie on the non‑sunny lines, and the remaining points \\(P_i\\) lie on the sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the problem exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (2.4) shows that no configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction of §3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
