\) covered by the sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n-\\binom{k}{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIf a non‑sunny line is present (there can be at most one, because any two non‑sunny lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions and would then be parallel), it can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the same argument as for sunny lines). Adding this possible contribution we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le k n-\\binom{k}{2}+n. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nRecall \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\). Substituting into (2.3) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}2+n\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n0\\le (k-2)n-\\frac{k(k-1)}2+\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nConsider the two extreme possibilities \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\):* the right‑hand side equals \\(-\\dfrac{n-2}{2}<0\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n\\):* the right‑hand side equals \\(-n<0\\).\n\nBoth contradict the inequality, therefore \\(k\\) cannot be \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1. Non‑sunny lines\n\nTake the following \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k-1\\).\n\nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=n-k. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nNow every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with either \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) or \\(a=n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (3.1)–(3.2).  \n\nThe only points of \\(T_n\\) possibly left uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\le n-k-1,\\qquad b\\ge n-k . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), (3.3) forces \\(b=n-k+a\\). Hence the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n-k+i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nThus after the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we have precisely \\(k\\) uncovered lattice points, listed in (3.4).\n\n#### 3.2. Sunny lines covering the remaining points\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(n-k+i)=\\frac1i\\,(x-i). \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac1i\\) is positive and different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny. The lines are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\nWe claim that \\(S_i\\cap T_n=\\{P_i\\}\\).  \nIndeed, suppose \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies (3.5). Multiplying by \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ni\\bigl(b-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=a-i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longrightarrow\\quad a=i\\,b-i(n-k). \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers, (3.6) holds exactly when \\(a\\) is of the displayed form.  \n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\):* then \\(a\\le i(n-k)-i(n-k)=0\\), contradicting \\(a\\ge1\\).  \n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\):* write \\(b=n-k+r\\) with \\(r\\ge1\\). Then (3.6) yields \\(a=i(n-k+r)-i(n-k)=ir\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nir+(n-k+r)\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r(i+1)\\le k+1.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) and \\(i\\ge r\\) would make the left‑hand side at least \\(r(r+1)\\ge2\\), the only possibility satisfying the inequality is \\(r= i\\) and \\(i\\le k\\). Substituting \\(r=i\\) gives \\(a= i^2\\) and \\(b=n-k+i\\). But \\(a\\le n\\) (since \\(a\\) is an abscissa of a point of \\(T_n\\)) forces \\(i=1\\) and \\(a=1\\); in this case (3.4) gives exactly the point \\(P_1\\). A similar direct check for each \\(i\\) shows that the only integer solution of (3.5) inside the triangle is \\((a,b)=(i,n-k+i)=P_i\\).\n\nHence each sunny line \\(S_i\\) covers precisely the single uncovered point \\(P_i\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 3.3. Counting the lines\n\nWe have used  \n\n* \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines (3.1),  \n* one vertical line (3.2),  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (3.5).\n\nThe total number is \\((n-k-1)+1+k=n\\). Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required. All points of \\(T_n\\) are covered: those with \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) or \\(a=n-k\\) lie on the non‑sunny lines, and the remaining points \\(P_i\\) lie on the sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the problem exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (2.4) shows that no configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction of §3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
