n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of integer lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nAll other lines (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) are called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L_1,L_2\\) be two distinct sunny lines. Then the set \\(L_1\\cap L_2\\cap T_n\\) contains **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equations of the lines as  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=m_jx+b_j\\qquad(j=1,2),\n\\]\n\nwhere each slope \\(m_j\\) is a rational number different from \\(0,-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\).  \nSince the slopes are distinct (otherwise the lines would be parallel and could not intersect), the two lines intersect in a unique point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) with  \n\n\\[\nx_0=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{m_1-m_2},\\qquad y_0=m_1x_0+b_1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth numerator and denominator are integers, hence \\(x_0,y_0\\) are rational numbers.  \nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in T_n\\) then, because the coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) are integers, we must have \\(x_0,y_0\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nThus at most one lattice point of \\(T_n\\) can belong to the intersection. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Points of \\(T_n\\) on a single line  \n\nFor **any** line (sunny or not) the inequality  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nholds, because a line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\)) in at most one point, and there are only \\(n\\) possible abscissae inside the triangle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Counting argument  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\},\\qquad\nm=n-k=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is non‑sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are only three forbidden directions, therefore  \n\n\\[\n0\\le m\\le 3. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nDenote the sunny lines by \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and the non‑sunny ones by \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_m\\).\n\nSumming the trivial bound (2.1) over all lines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}|L\\cap T_n|\\le kn+mn\\le kn+3n. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nIf two distinct sunny lines share a point of \\(T_n\\), that point has been counted twice in the sum (2.3).  \nBy Lemma 1 each unordered pair \\(\\{S_i,S_j\\}\\) can contribute **at most one** such double count.  \nHence the total number of double counts is at most \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\).\n\nConsequently the number of *distinct* points of \\(T_n\\) covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le kn+3n-\\binom{k}{2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1.1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn+3n-\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk^2-(2n-2)k+(n^2-2n)\\le 0,\n\\]\n\nwhose roots are \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).  \nSince the quadratic coefficient is positive, the inequality holds precisely for  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k\\ge n\\) is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total.  \nThus we have proved the **upper bound**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_j &: y=j \\qquad &&(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\nV   &: x=n-k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines together with the vertical line are distinct and belong to the three forbidden directions, hence they are non‑sunny.  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); every point with \\(a=n-k\\) lies on \\(V\\).  \nConsequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n-k+i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the remaining points  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) introduce the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(n-k+i)=\\frac{1}{n}\\,(x-i). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac1n\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nAll sunny lines have the same slope, so they are parallel but distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Each \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the point \\(P_i\\)\n\nWrite (3.2) in the equivalent integer form  \n\n\\[\nx-i=n\\bigl(y-(n-k+i)\\bigr). \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy (3.3). Then  \n\n\\[\na=i+n\\bigl(b-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=i+n(b-n+k-i)=i+n(b-n+k)-ni. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nSet \\(b=n-k+t\\) with an integer \\(t\\ge 0\\) (because \\(b\\ge n-k\\) follows from (3.3)).  \nSubstituting into (3.4) yields  \n\n\\[\na=i+nt. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nNow the condition \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+b=i+nt+n-k+t\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t(i+1)\\le k+1. \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) is a non‑negative integer, (3.6) forces \\(t\\) to be either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).  \n\n*If \\(t=0\\)*, then \\(b=n-k\\) and (3.5) gives \\(a=i\\).  \nBut \\(b=n-k\\) together with \\(a=i\\) violates the inequality \\(a\\ge1\\) together with the line equation (3.3) because substituting \\(b=n-k\\) into (3.3) yields \\(a=i-n\\cdot 0=i\\), which is admissible; however the point \\((i,n-k)\\) does **not** belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(a+b=i+n-k>n+1\\) when \\(i\\ge1