point.  Hence at most one point of \\(T_n\\) can lie on the intersection. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Points of \\(T_n\\) on a single line  \n\nFor any line \\(L\\) (sunny or not) we have  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n, \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nbecause the abscissa of a point of \\(T_n\\) can take only the values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\), and a line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Counting argument  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\},\\qquad\nm=n-k=\\#\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is non‑sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince there are only three forbidden directions, \\(0\\le m\\le 3\\).\n\nSumming (2.1) over all lines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}|L\\cap T_n|\\le kn+mn\\le kn+3n. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIf two distinct sunny lines share a point of \\(T_n\\), that point has been counted twice in the sum (2.2).  \nBy Lemma 1 each unordered pair of sunny lines can contribute **at most one** such double count; therefore the total number of double counts is at most \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\).\n\nConsequently the number of *distinct* points of \\(T_n\\) covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le kn+3n-\\binom{k}{2}. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1.1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn+3n-\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging yields  \n\n\\[\nk^2-(2n-2)k+(n^2-2n)\\le 0,\n\\]\n\nwhose roots are \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-2\\).  Because the quadratic coefficient is positive, the inequality holds precisely for  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k\\ge n\\) is impossible (we have only \\(n\\) lines), hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_j &: y=j \\qquad &&(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\nV   &: x=n-k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k-1\\) horizontal lines and the vertical line are non‑sunny.  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\); every point with \\(a=n-k\\) lies on \\(V\\).  \nHence the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are those with  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a\\le k .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(b=n-k+t\\) with \\(t\\ge 0\\) and using the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le k+1-t .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points form the set  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\Bigl\\{\\, (i,\\,n-k+j)\\;\\Big|\\;1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i \\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(U_k\\) is a right‑isosceles triangle of side length \\(k\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|U_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the remaining points  \n\nFor each pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i,j}:\\; n\\bigl(y-(n-k+j)\\bigr)=x-i . \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_{i,j}\\) equals \\(\\frac1n\\); therefore each \\(S_{i,j}\\) is sunny (it is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n*Claim.*  The line \\(S_{i,j}\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(T_n\\), namely \\((i,\\,n-k+j)\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nEquation (3.2) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nx\\equiv i \\pmod n . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nFor points of \\(T_n\\) the abscissa \\(x\\) ranges only over the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\); consequently (3.3) forces \\(x=i\\).  \nSubstituting \\(x=i\\) back into (3.2) yields \\(y=n-k+j\\).  \nThus the only lattice point of \\(T_n\\) satisfying (3.2) is \\((i,\\,n-k+j)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Selecting exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nBecause \\(|U_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\ge k\\) for every \\(k\\ge 0\\), we can choose any \\(k\\) distinct pairs \\((i,j)\\) from the index set \\(\\{1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\}\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_{i_1,j_1},\\dots ,S_{i_k,j_k}\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the corresponding \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy the claim each line of \\(\\mathcal S\\) covers a different point of \\(U_k\\); the remaining points of \\(U_k\\) (if any) are already covered by the non‑sunny lines because they lie on the vertical line \\(x=n-k\\) or on one of the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-k-1},V\\}\\cup\\mathcal S\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nSection 2 proved that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nSection 3 exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
