med by \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\) in at most two lattice points, so it could contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\nConsequently each point of \\(T_n\\) can be assigned a *private* line that passes through it and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\)\n\nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) choose a line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) that\n\n* passes through \\((a,b)\\);\n* does not pass through any other point of \\(T_n\\);\n* is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nSuch a line exists because, by Lemma 1, there are infinitely many lines through \\((a,b)\\) that avoid the other points of \\(T_n\\); among them at most three have a forbidden slope, so we can pick one with a permissible slope.  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\{\\ell_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny and they are pairwise distinct (each contains a different point of \\(T_n\\)).  \nBy (6),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|=|T_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  A configuration with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-3}:=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) consists of  \n\n\\[\n3+|T_n|=3+\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them (by construction of \\(T_n\\) and \\(\\mathcal S\\)).  \nExactly three lines (\\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\)) are non‑sunny, hence the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-3 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Obtaining configurations with more sunny lines  \n\nWe now replace one of the non‑sunny lines by a sunny line while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Replace the horizontal line.*  \nPick a point \\((a_0,h)\\in L_h\\) with \\(a_0\\ge 3\\) (such a point exists because \\(h\\le n\\)).  \nChoose any point \\((a_1,b_1)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell:=\\overline{(a_0,h)(a_1,b_1)}\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny. 