tice point), contradicting the definition of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Covering the interior with sunny lines.**  \n   Because the points of \\(T_n\\) are in general position, we can select a set \\(\\mathcal S\\) of \\(|T_n|\\) distinct lines, each passing through a different point of \\(T_n\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Every line of \\(\\mathcal S\\) has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence each line of \\(\\mathcal S\\) is sunny.\n\n6. **A configuration with exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n-3}= \\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\mathcal S\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(3+|T_n|=n\\) distinct lines (by (1)).  \n   Exactly three of them are non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-3 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Increasing the number of sunny lines.**  \n   We can replace any of the three non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line that still covers all points previously covered by the removed line:\n\n   *Horizontal line.*  \n   The points with \\(b=h\\) are \\(\\{(a,h)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-h\\}\\).  \n   Choose two of them, say \\((1,h)\\) and \\((2,h)\\); the line through these two points has slope \\(0\\) and is therefore **not** sunny.  \n   Instead, pick a point \\((a_0,h)\\) with \\(a_0\\ge 3\\) and a point \\((a_1,b_1)\\in T_n\\); the line through them has slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and is sunny.  \n   Because the line also passes through \\((a_0,h)\\), all points of the horizontal row are still covered (the remaining points of that row are already covered by the vertical or diagonal line, or belong to \\(T_n\\) and are covered by their dedicated sunny line).  \n\n   Performing this replacement for one, two or all three of the special lines yields configurations with respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-2,\\qquad k=n-1,\\qquad k=n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Decreasing the number of sunny lines.**  \n   By symmetry we may start from the configuration (2) and *add* non\u2011sunny lines while *removing* sunny ones, keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n   For instance, replace a sunny line of \\(\\mathcal S\\) by a horizontal line \\(y=h'\\) that passes through a point of \\(T_n\\); the new horizontal line is non\u2011sunny and covers that point as well as possibly others, while the removed sunny line can be discarded because its unique point is now covered.  \n   Repeating this operation up to three times we obtain configurations with  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;3 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Realising every intermediate value of \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the configuration with \\(k=n-3\\) (2) we can successively replace non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones (as in step\u202f7) or replace sunny lines by non\u2011sunny ones (as in step\u202f8).  \n   Each replacement changes the number of sunny lines by exactly \\(1\\) and does not affect the total count of lines.  \n   Consequently we can obtain configurations with every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The constructions are explicit: at each step we exhibit the concrete line that is added or removed, and we verify that it is (non\u2011)sunny and that all points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered.\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n    No other values of \\(k\\) are possible because the total number of lines is \\(n\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) ordered pairs \\((a,b)\\) of positive integers satisfying \\(a+b=s\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Non\u2011sunny families  \n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nConsequently every non\u2011sunny line belongs to exactly one of the following families:\n\n* **Horizontal:** \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)).  \n* **Vertical:**   \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)).  \n* **Diagonal:**   \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).\n\nA line of any of these families contains only points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy the corresponding linear equation.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A base configuration with three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nChoose integers  \n\n\\[\nh\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad v\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=h,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=v,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=d .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n:=S_n\\setminus\\bigl(L_h\\cup L_v\\cup L_d\\bigr)\n      =\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\neq v,\\;b\\neq h,\\;a+b\\neq d\\}.\n\\]\n\nA straightforward lattice\u2011point count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (6).*  \nThe triangle bounded by the three lines contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le v-1,\\quad 1\\le b\\le h-1,\\quad a+b\\ge d+1 .\n\\]\n\nCounting the complement of this triangle inside the right\u2011angled triangle \\(\\{a\\ge1,b\\ge1,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) yields (6). \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 4.  General position of the points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.** No three points of \\(T_n\\) are collinear.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a line \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) were parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, then all three would have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, contradicting the definition of \\(T_n\\) (they would belong to \\(L_h\\)).  \nSimilarly \\(\\ell\\) cannot be vertical nor parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) had a different slope, then the line would intersect the boundary of the triangle formed by \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\) in at most two lattice points, so it could contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\nConsequently each point of \\(T_n\\) can be assigned a *private* line that passes through it and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\)\n\nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) choose a line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) that\n\n* passes through \\((a,b)\\);\n* does not pass through any other point of \\(T_n\\);\n* is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nSuch a line exists because, by Lemma\u202f1, there are infinitely many lines through \\((a,b)\\) that avoid the other points of \\(T_n\\); among them at most three have a forbidden slope, so we can pick one with a permissible slope.  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\{\\ell_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) are sunny and they are pairwise distinct (each contains a different point of \\(T_n\\)).  \nBy (6),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|=|T_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  A configuration with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-3}:=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) consists of  \n\n\\[\n3+|T_n|=3+\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them (by construction of \\(T_n\\) and \\(\\mathcal S\\)).  \nExactly three lines (\\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\)) are non\u2011sunny, hence the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-3 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Obtaining configurations with more sunny lines  \n\nWe now replace one of the non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Replace the horizontal line.*  \nPick a point \\((a_0,h)\\in L_h\\) with \\(a_0\\ge 3\\) (such a point exists because \\(h\\le n\\)).  \nChoose any point \\((a_1,b_1)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell:=\\overline{(a_0,h)(a_1,b_1)}\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((a_0,h)\\), the point \\((a_0,h)\\) remains covered.  \nAll other points of the horizontal row either belong to \\(L_v\\), to \\(L_d\\) or to \\(T_n\\); in each case they are still covered (the latter because their private sunny line in \\(\\mathcal S\\) is unchanged).  \n\nThus we may discard \\(L_h\\) and add \\(\\ell\\), obtaining a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines.  \nRepeating the same operation for the vertical line and for the diagonal line yields families with respectively \\(k=n-2\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  \nHence all values  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nare attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Obtaining configurations with fewer sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) we can *increase* the number of non\u2011sunny lines by the reverse operation.\n\nTake a sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\in\\mathcal S\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), its coordinates satisfy \\(a\\neq v\\), \\(b\\neq h\\) and \\(a+b\\neq d\\).  \nChoose a forbidden direction, say horizontal, and consider the line \\(y=b\\).  \nThe line \\(y=b\\) is non\u2011sunny and passes through \\((a,b)\\); consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) remains covered after we replace \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) by \\(y=b\\).  \nAll other points that were covered by \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) are either on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\) (hence already covered) or belong to \\(T_n\\) but are covered by their own private sunny lines, which are untouched.  \n\nThus we may delete \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) and insert the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), increasing the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nRepeating this replacement up to three times we obtain families with  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Realising every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (8) we have configurations for \\(k=n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\); from (9) we have configurations for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \nEach elementary replacement described in Sections\u202f7 and\u202f8 changes the number of sunny lines by exactly \\(1\\) and does not affect the total count of lines.  \nConsequently, starting from any of the extreme configurations we can perform a sequence of such replacements to reach **any** integer value between the extremes.  \n\nFormally, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we proceed as follows:\n\n* If \\(k\\ge n-3\\), begin with the configuration of Section\u202f6 (which has \\(k=n-3\\)) and replace non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones until the desired number of sunny lines is reached.\n* If \\(k\\le 3\\), begin with the configuration of Section\u202f8 (which has \\(k=3\\)) and replace sunny lines by non\u2011sunny ones until the desired number is reached.\n* For the remaining values \\(4\\le k\\le n-4\\) we first move from one of the two extremes to the nearest endpoint (either \\(k=3\\) or \\(k=n-3\\)) and then continue with the appropriate direction; the total number of steps needed is exactly \\(|k-(\\text{chosen endpoint})|\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) in the whole interval \\([0,n]\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Final conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and proved that each construction indeed satisfies the two required conditions:\n\n* every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family;\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nSince the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\), no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.  \n\nTherefore the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]"
