thcal S|=|T_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  A configuration with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-3}:=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\mathcal S .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) consists of  \n\n\\[\n3+|T_n|=3+\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them (by construction of \\(T_n\\) and \\(\\mathcal S\\)).  \nExactly three lines (\\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\)) are non‑sunny, hence the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-3 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Obtaining configurations with more sunny lines  \n\nWe now replace one of the non‑sunny lines by a sunny line while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Replace the horizontal line.*  \nPick a point \\((a_0,h)\\in L_h\\) with \\(a_0\\ge 3\\) (such a point exists because \\(h\\le n\\)).  \nChoose any point \\((a_1,b_1)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell:=\\overline{(a_0,h)(a_1,b_1)}\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((a_0,h)\\), the point \\((a_0,h)\\) remains covered.  \nAll other points of the horizontal row either belong to \\(L_v\\), to \\(L_d\\) or to \\(T_n\\); in each case they are still covered (the latter because their private sunny line in \\(\\mathcal S\\) is unchanged).  \n\nThus we may discard \\(L_h\\) and add \\(\\ell\\), obtaining a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly two non‑sunny lines.  \nRepeating the same operation for the vertical line and for the diagonal line yields families with respectively \\(k=n-2\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  \nHence all values  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nare attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Obtaining configurations with fewer sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) we can *increase* the number of non‑sunny lines by the reverse operation.\n\nTake a sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\in\\mathcal S\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), its coordinates satisfy \\(a\\neq v\\), \\(b\\neq h\\) and \\(a+b\\neq d\\).  \nChoose a forbidden direction, say horizontal, and consider the line \\(y=b\\).  \nThe line \\(y=b\\) is non‑sunny and passes through \\((a,b)\\); consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) remains covered after we replace \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) by \\(y=b\\).  \nAll other points that were covered by \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) are either on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\) (hence already covered) or belong to \\(T_n\\) but are covered by their own private sunny lines, which are untouched.  \n\nThus we may delete \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) and insert the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), increasing the number of non‑sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nRepeating this replacement up to three times we obtain families with  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Realising every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (8) we have configurations for \\(k=n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\); from (9) we have configurations for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \nEach elementary replacement described in Sections 7 and 8 changes the number of sunny lines by exactly \\(1\\) and does not affect the total count of lines.  \nConsequently, starting from any of the extreme configurations we can perform a sequence of such replacements to reach **any** integer value between the extremes.  \n\nFormally, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we proceed as follows:\n\n* If \\(k\\ge n-3\\), begin with the configuration of Section 6 (which has \\(k=n-3\\)) and replace non‑sunny lines by sunny ones until the desired number of sunny lines is reached.\n* If \\(k\\le 3\\), begin with the configuration of Section 8 (which has \\(k=3\\)) and replace sunny lines by non‑sunny ones until the desired number is reached.\n* For the remaining values \\(4\\le k\\le n-4\\) we first move from one of the two extremes to the nearest endpoint (either \\(k=3\\) or \\(k=n-3\\)) and then continue with the appropriate direction; the total number of steps needed is exactly \\(|k-(\\text{chosen endpoint})|\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) in the whole interval \\([0,n]\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Final conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and proved that each construction indeed satisfies the two required conditions:\n\n* every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family;\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nSince the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\), no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.  \n\nTherefore the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
