ce \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((a_0,h)\\), the point \\((a_0,h)\\) remains covered.  \nAll other points of the horizontal row either belong to \\(L_v\\), to \\(L_d\\) or to \\(T_n\\); in each case they are still covered (the latter because their private sunny line in \\(\\mathcal S\\) is unchanged).  \n\nThus we may discard \\(L_h\\) and add \\(\\ell\\), obtaining a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly two non‑sunny lines.  \nRepeating the same operation for the vertical line and for the diagonal line yields families with respectively \\(k=n-2\\), \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  \nHence all values  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nare attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Obtaining configurations with fewer sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) we can *increase* the number of non‑sunny lines by the reverse operation.\n\nTake a sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\in\\mathcal S\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), its coordinates satisfy \\(a\\neq v\\), \\(b\\neq h\\) and \\(a+b\\neq d\\).  \nChoose a forbidden direction, say horizontal, and consider the line \\(y=b\\).  \nThe line \\(y=b\\) is non‑sunny and passes through \\((a,b)\\); consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) remains covered after we replace \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) by \\(y=b\\).  \nAll other points that were covered by \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) are either on \\(L_h\\), \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_d\\) (hence already covered) or belong to \\(T_n\\) but are covered by their own private sunny lines, which are untouched.  \n\nThus we may delete \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) and insert the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), increasing the number of non‑sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nRepeating this replacement up to three times we obtain families with  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Realising every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (8) we have configurations for \\(k=n-3,n-2,n-1,n\\); from (9) we have configurations for 