o three points of \\(T_n\\) are collinear.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a line \\(\\ell\\).  \n   If \\(\\ell\\) were horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\) then all its points would satisfy respectively \\(b=h\\), \\(a=v\\) or \\(a+b=d\\), contradicting the definition of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The three lines \\(L_h,L_v,L_d\\) bound a right‑angled triangle \\(\\Delta\\) with vertices \\((v,0),(0,h),(v,d-v)\\).  \n   Any line of slope different from the three forbidden slopes meets the boundary of \\(\\Delta\\) in at most two lattice points; consequently it can contain at most two lattice points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Therefore \\(\\ell\\) cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n   Consequently each point of \\(T_n\\) admits a *private* line that passes through it and through no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Sunny lines covering the points of \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n   For a given \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) consider all lines through \\((a,b)\\).  \n   Among these lines at most three have a forbidden slope (\\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Because there are infinitely many lines through \\((a,b)\\), we can choose one, call it \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\), that\n\n   * avoids every other point of \\(T_n\\) (possible by Lemma 1), and  \n   * has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   Hence each \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) is sunny and the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S:=\\{\\ell_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(|T_n|\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n6. **A base configuration with exactly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.**  \n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n-3}:=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\mathcal S .\n   \\]\n\n   By (1) the number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   3+|T_n|=3+\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\): the points on the three special lines are covered by them, the remaining points are exactly the elements of \\(T_n\\) and each of them lies on its private sunny line.  \n   Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) satisfies the required covering condition and contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-3\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines.\n\n7. **Increasing the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   We replace a non‑sunny line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property.\n\n   *Horizontal line.*  \n   Choose a point \\((a_0,h)\\in L_h\\) with \\(a_0\\ge 3\\) (such a point exists because \\(h\\le n\\)).  \n   Pick any \\((a_1,b_1)\\in T_n\\) and let \\(\\ell:=\\overline{(a_0,h)(a_1,b_1)}\\).  \n   By construction \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction, hence sunny.  \n   The point \\((a_0,h)\\) remains covered (by \\(\\ell\\)).  \n   Any other point \\((a,h)\\) of the horizontal row satisfies one of the following:\n\n   * \\(a=v\\) – then it lies on \\(L_v\\);  \n   * \\(a+b=d\\) – then it lies on \\(L_d\\);  \n   * otherwise \\((a,h)\\in T_n\\) and is covered by its private sunny line in \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n   Hence after discarding \\(L_h\\) and adding \\(\\ell\\) all points of \\(S_n\\) stay covered.  \n   The number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\n   The same argument works for the vertical line \\(L_v\\) and for the diagonal line \\(L_d\\).  \n   Performing the replacement for one, two or all three of the special lines yields configurations with respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-2,\\qquad k=n-1,\\qquad k=n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Decreasing the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Starting again from \\(\\mathcal L_{n-3}\\) we can turn a sunny line into a non‑sunny one.\n\n   Let \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\in\\mathcal S\\) be the private sunny line of the point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   Consider the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (the argument is identical for a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\)).  \n   The line \\(y=b\\) is non‑sunny and passes through \\((a,b)\\); therefore after deleting \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\)