e covered is\n\n\\[\n2(n-m)+2(m-1)=2n-2<2n-1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the fact that all \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(R\\cup C\\) must be covered.  \nHence any covering of \\(T_n\\) must contain **at least \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines**. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of the admissible numbers of sunny lines\n\nFrom Lemma 2 the number of non‑sunny lines is at least \\(m\\); therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-m\n   = n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConversely, let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nStart with the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of Lemma 1, which consists of exactly \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and already covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nAdd the remaining \\(n-m\\) lines arbitrarily, choosing exactly \\(k\\) of them to have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (these are sunny) and the other \\(n-m-k\\) to be, for instance, horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) with distinct constants \\(c\\) not used before.  \nSince the original \\(m\\) lines already provide a covering, the addition of extra lines does not affect the covering condition, and the total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) can be realised, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe upper bound follows from Lemma 2 and inequality (3).  \nThe lower bound (realisation) follows from the construction described after Lemma 2. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-b