(L_i\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) are already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The remaining points\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) as \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and using \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(n-k+1,k),\\;(n-k+2,k-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=k+(k-1)+\\dots +1=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y = -\\frac{j}{\\,k+1-j\\,}\\,x + \\frac{(k+1)j}{\\,k+1-j\\,}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*The slope* of \\(M_j\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{j}{k+1-j}\\).  \nSince \\(j\\ge 1\\) and \\(k+1-j\\ge 1\\), this slope is never \\(0\\) (so the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), never undefined (so it is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and never \\(-1\\) (so it is not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)). Consequently every \\(M_j\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\(M_j\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_j:=\\bigl(n-k+j,\\;k+1-j\\bigr),\\qquad\nQ_j:=\\bigl(n-k+j+1,\\;k-j\\bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to the set \\(S_k\\) (they appear consecutively in the list (5)).  \nConversely, each point of \\(S_k\\) appears in exactly one of the pairs \\(\\{P_j,Q_j\\}\\) (the first point of the list is \\(P_1\\), the last point is \\(Q_k\\)).  \nTherefore the family \\(\\{M_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) covers all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\nThe slopes in (7) are distinct for different values of \\(j\\); hence the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The whole family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nCollecting (3) and (7) we obtain the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_k:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(M_j\\) are sunny.  \nBy the discussion in §§2.1–2.3 every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) a configuration satisfying the two requirements of the problem exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nWe have shown that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.  \nTogether with the trivial bound (2) this proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the answer is**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
