dden values. Two distinct sunny lines intersect in a unique point. Because there are only \\(n\\) lines, at most \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) intersection points can be produced, and each intersection lies on exactly two lines. The total number of incidences (point\u2013line memberships) contributed by intersections is therefore at most \\(2\\binom{n}{2}=n(n-1)\\).  \n\n   However the set of required points has cardinality\n   \\[\n   |\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   which exceeds \\(n(n-1)\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence the covering cannot be achieved with only sunny lines.\n\n   *If there is exactly one non\u2011sunny line*, that line can contain at most \\(n\\) of the required points (its intersection with the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is a line segment of length at most \\(n\\)). The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. By the same counting argument as above, the maximum number of points that \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover is\n   \\[\n   (n-1)(n-2)+ (n-1)= (n-1)(n-1)= (n-1)^2,\n   \\]\n   which is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line is also impossible.\n\n   Therefore configurations with \\(k=n\\) (all sunny) or \\(k=n-1\\) (exactly one non\u2011sunny) cannot exist.\n\nCombining the constructive part (step\u202f2) with the impossibility proof (step\u202f3) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_c\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the above range, so it lies on the corresponding \\(L_c\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines \\(\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) cover \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall replace the diagonal line \\(L_{t+2}\\) by a sunny line that still covers all points of \\(L_{t+2}\\cap T_n\\).\n\nThe set \\(L_{t+2}\\cap T_n\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1,\\ a+b=t+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese points form a contiguous segment of the lattice with endpoints \\((1,t+1)\\) and \\((t+1,1)\\).  \nChoose the slope \\(m=2\\) (any rational number different from \\(0,-1\\) would work) and define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = 2x + (t-2).\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le t+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = 2a + (t-2) = a + (a+t-2) .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+t-2 = (t+2)-a\\), we obtain \\(b = t+2 - a\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on both \\(L_{t+2}\\) and \\(S_t\\).  \nConsequently \\(S_t\\) contains **exactly** the same lattice points of \\(T_n\\) as \\(L_{t+2}\\).\n\nThe line \\(S_t\\) is sunny (its slope \\(2\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)), and it is distinct from all the remaining diagonal lines \\(L_c\\) (\\(c\\neq t+2\\)).  \n\nReplacing \\(L_{t+2}\\) by \\(S_t\\) therefore yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(T_n\\) and now contains one sunny line. Repeating the replacement for any \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(c\\) (with \\(k\\le n-2\\)) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nHence for every integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at most one non\u2011sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1.  No non\u2011sunny line (\\(k=n\\))\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny, i.e. have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nTwo distinct sunny lines intersect in a unique point, and no three distinct sunny lines can pass through the same point (otherwise two of them would be parallel, contradicting distinctness of slopes).  \n\nThus the total number of incidences (pairs \u201cpoint \\(\\in\\) line\u201d) contributed by intersections is at most  \n\n\\[\n2\\binom{n}{2}=n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) must belong to at least one line, so we need at least \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) incidences.  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n(n-1),\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2.  Exactly one non\u2011sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the unique non\u2011sunny line. Because \\(L\\) is parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\), its intersection with the triangle \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is a line segment containing at most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. As in the previous sub\u2011case, the maximal number of incidences that \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can provide is  \n\n\\[\n2\\binom{n-1}{2} + (n-1) = (n-1)(n-2)+(n-1) = (n-1)^2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n(n-1)^2 < \\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nso the required points cannot all be covered. This contradiction shows that a configuration with e