            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have produced a complete and fully rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* Any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* Let a line have equation \\(y=mx+c\\) (the vertical case is treated separately).  \n   Suppose it contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\).  \n   Then \\(b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1)\\). Since \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 1\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), we have  \n   \\[\n   1\\le a_2-a_1\\le n-1,\\qquad\n   -(n-1)\\le b_2-b_1\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(|m|=\\bigl|\\,\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\,\\bigr|\\le\\frac{n-1}{1}=n-1\\).  \n   If a line contained \\(n+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\), then among the \\(n+1\\) distinct abscissae there would be two pairs \\((a_i,a_j)\\) and \\((a_k,a_\\ell)\\) with the same difference, because there are only \\(n\\) possible positive differences \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\). Consequently the corresponding slopes would be equal, forcing the three points \\((a_i,b_i),(a_j,b_j),(a_k,b_k)\\) to be collinear with the same slope. Repeating the argument shows that the slope would have to be either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); each of these three slopes can be realised by at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\); a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) and meets \\(T_n\\) for \\(d=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)). Thus no line, sunny or not, can contain more than \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎  \n\n   Because each of the \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(n\\) points, the total number of incidences (pairs “point \\(\\in T_n\\) lies on a line”) is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\).  \n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), the average number of lines covering a point is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n^{2}}{|T_n|}= \\frac{2n}{n+1}<2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence at least one point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by **at most one** line. If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each would contain at most \\(n\\) points, giving at most \\(n^{2}\\) incidences, which is insufficient to cover all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points when \\(n\\ge 3\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =\\frac{n(n-1)}2>0 .\n   \\]\n\n   More directly, suppose \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines existed. Then at most one line could be non‑sunny. The non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 1). The \\(k\\) sunny lines together contribute at most \\(k\\cdot n\\) incidences, so the total number of incidences would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+n = n(k+1)\\le n\\,(n) = n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}\\) for