ertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) in at most one lattice‑segment, and a simple counting of integer abscissae shows that no more than \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on it. ∎  \n\n   Because each of the \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(n\\) points, the total number of incidences (point–line pairs) is at most \\(n^{2}\\). Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), at least one point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by **exactly one** line. If \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines existed, then at most one line would be non‑sunny; the non‑sunny line also contains at most \\(n\\) points, so the total number of incidences would be at most  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+n = n(k+1)\\le n\\cdot n = n^{2},\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the necessity of more than \\(n^{2}\\) incidences. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Idea.*  \n   We will use two non‑sunny lines (one vertical, one horizontal) to cover the “border” of the triangle \\(T_n\\) and \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) to cover the interior. The sunny lines will be chosen so that each of them contains exactly \\(n-k\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the two non‑sunny lines will together contain the remaining \\(2k\\) points, giving a total of \\(k(n-k)+2k = kn+2k = n(k+2)-2k\\) incidences, which equals \\(|T_n|\\) because \\(k\\le n-2\\). The construction is explicit and works for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n   **Step 1 – the two non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{V}:\\;x=n-k+1 ,\\qquad   \n   L_{H}:\\;y=n-k+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non‑sunny (vertical and horizontal).  \n   Their intersections with \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{V}\\cap T_n=\\{(n-k+1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L_{H}\\cap T_n=\\{(a,n-k+1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the two lines together cover exactly \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   **Step 2 – the sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = x + (i-1). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(S_i\\) is sunny (slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\n   **Step 3 – points of \\(T_n\\) lying on a sunny line.**  \n   Fix \\(i\\) and let  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i,a}:=\\bigl(a,\\;a+i-1\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification that \\(P_{i,a}\\in T_n\\).*  \n\n   – Clearly \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+i-1\\ge 1\\) because \\(i\\ge 1\\).  \n\n   – Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(a+i-1)=2a+i-1\\le 2(n-k)+k-1 = 2n-2k+k-1 = 2n-k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(2n-k-1\\le n+1\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(a+i-1)\\le n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   proving that \\(P_{i,a}\\in T_n\\).  \n\n   Consequently each sunny line \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the \\(n-k\\) points \\(\\{P_{i,a}\\mid a=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).\n\n   **Step 4 – disjointness.**  \n   For a fixed abscissa \\(a\\) the second coordinate on different sunny lines differs by the amount \\((i-j)\\); therefore the sets \\(\\{P_{i,a}\\mid a\\}\\) for distinct \\(i\\) are pairwise disjoint. Moreover none of the points in (2)–(3) lies on a sunny line: if \\((n-k+1,b)=P_{i,a}\\) then \\(a=n-k+1\\) and \\(b=a+i-1=n-k+i\\ge n-k+1\\), contradicting \\(b\\le k\\) (recall \\(k\\le n-2\\)). A symmetric argument excludes the points of (3). Hence the three families of points are mutually disjoint.\n\n   **Step 5 – completeness of the covering.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n   *Case 1:* \\(a\\le n-k\\). Then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Write \\(b-a+1 = i\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) (the inequality \\(i\\le k\\) follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\)). Hence \\((a,b)=P_{i,a}\\) and the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  \n\n   *Case 2:* \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k\\).  \n\n   – If \\(a=n-k+1\\) then