e reduced denominator divides both \\(a_2-a_1\\) and \\(a_3-a_2\\); therefore it divides their greatest common divisor \\(d:=\\gcd(a_2-a_1,a_3-a_2)\\).\n\nBecause \\(a_1\\ge1\\) and \\(a_3\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a_3+b_3>n+1\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\n0< a_2-a_1\\le n-1,\\qquad 0< a_3-a_2\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(d\\le n-1\\).  \nNow write \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms (\\(q>0\\)).  \nFrom (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1 = m(a_2-a_1)=\\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1),\\qquad\nb_3-b_2 = m(a_3-a_2)=\\frac{p}{q}(a_3-a_2).\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand sides are integers, \\(q\\) must divide both \\(a_2-a_1\\) and \\(a_3-a_2\\); hence \\(q\\mid d\\).  \nThus \\(q\\le d\\le n-1\\).\n\nNow use the condition \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  From \\(b_i = m a_i + b\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na_i + m a_i + b \\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a_i \\le n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points have distinct \\(a_i\\), the left\u2011hand side takes three distinct values, which is impossible unless \\(1+m=0\\) (i.e. \\(m=-1\\)) or \\(m=0\\).  \nThe case \\(m=\\infty\\) (vertical line) is excluded by hypothesis.  \n\nHence a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) cannot contain three points of \\(S\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; they may cover any number of points, but the total number of covered points cannot exceed \\(|S|\\) given by (1).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n2k \\ge |S| - (n-k)\\cdot 0 = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) the right\u2011hand side is larger than \\(2n\\); consequently the inequality cannot hold when \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \nA sharper observation is that a non\u2011sunny line must be parallel to one of the three directions \\(x=\\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\) or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \nOnly three lines can be chosen with distinct directions among these families, so at most three lines can be non\u2011sunny.  \nSince \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), three non\u2011sunny lines are insufficient to cover all points; therefore **at least two** lines must be non\u2011sunny.  \n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that together contain all points of \\(S\\).\n\n#### 4.1.  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n-i-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), hence belong to \\(S\\).  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n-i-1)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-intercepts, and each contains exactly the two points \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\).  \nThe sets \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, since the first coordinates of the points are all different.\n\nThus the sunny lines cover precisely the \\(2k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\subset S .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2.  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nConsider the remaining points  \n\n\\[\nT:=S\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\nFor any column \\(x=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n\\) at most one point of that column belongs to \\(U\\) (namely \\(P_j\\) or \\(Q_{j-1}\\) when such an index exists).  \nHence every column still contains at least one point of \\(T\\).  \n\nWe now use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny (they are