ble \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points that must be covered  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions of\n\\(a+b=s\\) in positive integers; hence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1  \n\n**Statement.**  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) then \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le 2\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) (the vertical case is excluded).  \nSuppose \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S\\) with\n\\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nBecause the points lie on \\(\\ell\\),\n\n\\[\nb_i=m a_i+b\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\nb_3-b_2=m(a_3-a_2). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(a_i,b_i\\) are positive integers, therefore the left\u2011hand sides of (2) and (3) are integers.  \nConsequently \\(m\\) must be rational; write \\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms\nwith \\(q>0\\).\n\nFrom (2) we obtain \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\) and from (3) \\(q\\mid (a_3-a_2)\\); hence\n\\(q\\) divides their greatest common divisor \\(d:=\\gcd(a_2-a_1,a_3-a_2)\\).\nThus \\(q\\le d\\).\n\nNow use the defining inequality of \\(S\\):\n\\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  Substituting \\(b_i=ma_i+b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na_i+ma_i+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a_i\\le n+1-b .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\neq -1\\) then \\(1+m\\neq0\\) and (4) bounds each \\(a_i\\) from above by the same\nconstant \\(\\dfrac{n+1-b}{1+m}\\).  Since the three numbers \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\) are distinct,\nthey cannot all satisfy the same strict upper bound unless the bound is at least\n\\(a_3\\).  However (4) together with \\(a_3\\ge a_2+1\\ge a_1+2\\) forces \\(q\\le 1\\),\nwhich would make \\(m\\) an integer.  The only integer slopes allowed for a line\nthrough three points of \\(S\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) (the latter corresponds to the\nforbidden slope).  Because we assumed \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), a contradiction\narises.\n\nHence a line with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) cannot contain three points\nof \\(S\\); therefore \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S\\) and let\n\\(k\\) of them be sunny.\n\n*Each sunny line* contributes at most two points of \\(S\\) by Lemma\u202f1, so the sunny\nlines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\)\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\): indeed, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets\n\\(S\\) in the points \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\), a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) in\n\\((1,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\), and a line \\(x+y=c\\) in \\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\);\nin each case the number of points is at most \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently, if at most one line of the family is non\u2011sunny, the total number\nof points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2k+n .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(k=n-1\\) this bound equals \\(3n-2\\), and when \\(k=n\\) it equals \\(3n\\).\nBoth numbers are strictly smaller than \\(|S|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\) (the inequality \\(3n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) holds for \\(n\\ge4\\), and a\ndirect check shows it also holds for \\(n=3\\)).  Hence at least two lines must be\nnon\u2011sunny, which yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 4.1.  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n-i-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so they belong to \\(S\\).  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n-i-1)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are distinct (their \\(x\\)-intercepts\nare \\(i-\\frac{n+1-i}{2}\\), all different) and each contains exactly the two\npoints \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\).  \nThus the sunny lines cover the set  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i\\},\\qquad |U|=2k .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2.  Non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT:=S\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\nIn each column \\(x=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) at most one point belongs to \\(U\\)\n(the point \\(P_j\\) when \\(j\\le k\\) or the point \\(Q_{j-1}\\) when \\(j-1\\le k\\));\nhence every column still contains at least one point of \\(T\\).\n\nWe use the following \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\\);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n(When \\(k=n-2\\) we have exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, namely \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\);\nwhen \\(k<n-2\\) the list contains more vertical lines, all distinct.)\n\n**Coverage of \\(T\\).**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k-1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is in our\nlist.\n\n*If \\(a\\ge n-k\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\).\nHence \\(b\\in\\{1,2\\}\\).\n\n- If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n- If \\(b=2\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\). Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(a\\le n-k-1\\);\n  consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is among the chosen vertical\n  lines) contains the point.\n\nThus every point of \\(T\\) belongs to at least one of the selected non\u2011sunny\nlines.  All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly