 the total number of\ncovered points is at most \\(2k+n\\).  Substituting \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) gives\n\\(2(n-1)+n=3n-2\\) and \\(2n+n=3n\\), respectively.  Both numbers are strictly\nsmaller than \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (the inequality\n\\(3n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) holds for \\(n\\ge4\\) and can be checked directly for\n\\(n=3\\)).  Therefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny, which yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 4.1.  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n-i-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so they belong to \\(S\\).  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n-i-1)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are distinct (they have different\n\\(x\\)-intercepts) and each contains exactly the two points \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\).  \nThus the sunny lines cover  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i\\},\\qquad |U|=2k .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2.  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT:=S\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\n*Observation.* In each column \\(x=j\\;(1\\le j\\le n)\\) at most one point of that\ncolumn lies in \\(U\\) (the point \\(P_j\\) when \\(j\\le k\\) or the point\n\\(Q_{j-1}\\) when \\(j-1\\le k\\)).  Hence every column still contains at least one\npoint of \\(T\\).\n\nWe now introduce exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\\)   (\\(n-k-1\\) lines);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)                 (1 line);\n* the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) (1 line).\n\nThe total number of non‑sunny lines is \\((n-k-1)+1+1=n-k\\), as required.\n\n#### 4.3.  Verification of coverage  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k-1\\).*  \nThe point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is among the chosen vertical\nlines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\ge n-k\\).*  \nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\); thus\n\\(b\\in\\{1,2\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n* If \\(b=2\\) then \\(a=n-1\\) (because \\(a+2\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the\n  only way to have \\(b=2\\) while remaining in \\(T\\) is \\(a=n-1\\); any smaller\n  \\(a\\) would give a point already covered by a sunny line).  \n  The point \\((n-1,2)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\), hence it lies on the line\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nTogether with the sunny lines, all points of \\(S\\) are covered.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the required\nproperty holds.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 together with the counting argument forces \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\nsuch a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
