            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points that must be covered  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions of\n\\(a+b=s\\) in positive integers; consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1  \n\n**Statement.**  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) then \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le 2\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) (the vertical case is excluded).  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in S\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nBecause the points lie on \\(\\ell\\),\n\n\\[\nb_i=ma_i+b\\qquad(i=1,2,3). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince the left‑hand side of (3) is an integer, \\(m\\) must be rational; write\n\\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).\n\nEquation (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\nq\\,(b_2-b_1)=p\\,(a_2-a_1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), (4) implies \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\).  \nSimilarly, from \\(b_3-b_2=m(a_3-a_2)\\) we obtain \\(q\\mid (a_3-a_2)\\).  \nHence \\(q\\) divides the greatest common divisor\n\\(d:=\\gcd(a_2-a_1,\\;a_3-a_2)\\).  In particular \\(q\\le d\\).\n\nNow use the defining inequality of \\(S\\).  From (2) we have  \n\n\\[\na_i+ma_i+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a_i\\le n+1-b .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq -1\\), the factor \\(1+m\\) is non‑zero.  Moreover \\(m\\) is not\n\\(0\\), so \\(|1+m|\\ge 1\\).  Hence (5) yields the uniform bound  \n\n\\[\na_i\\le \\frac{n+1-b}{|1+m|}\\le n .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus each \\(a_i\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  Since the three\n\\(a_i\\) are distinct, we have  \n\n\\[\na_3-a_1\\ge 2 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(a_3-a_1=(a_3-a_2)+(a_2-a_1)\\) is a sum of two positive multiples of \\(q\\)\n(because \\(q\\mid a_2-a_1\\) and \\(q\\mid a_3-a_2\\)).  Consequently \\(q\\) divides\n\\(a_3-a_1\\).  From (7) we obtain \\(q\\le a_3-a_1\\le n-1\\).\n\nIf \\(q\\ge 2\\) then \\(m\\) is a non‑integer rational number.  Returning to (5) and\nusing \\(|1+m|\\ge 1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\na_3\\le \\frac{n+1-b}{|1+m|}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a_3\\ge a_1+2\\ge 3\\), the three distinct integers \\(a_1,a_2,a_3\\)\nmust occupy three different residue classes modulo \\(q\\).  This is impossible\nwhen \\(q\\ge 2\\) and the interval \\([1,n]\\) contains at most \\(n\\) integers while\nthe three numbers already occupy three distinct classes; the pigeon‑hole\nprinciple forces \\(q=1\\).\n\nThus \\(q=1\\) and the slope \\(m\\) is an integer.  The only integer slopes that\nallow three points of \\(S\\) are \\(0\\) (horizontal) and \\(-1\\) (the line\n\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)), both excluded by the hypothesis.  Hence our assumption\nthat three points lie on a sunny line is false, and the lemma follows. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S\\) and\nlet exactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.\n\n* By Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore the\n  sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\n* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line\n  \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (the intersection consists of the\n  points of a single row, a single column, or a single diagonal, each of length\n  at most \\(n\\)).\n\nHence, if at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny, the total number of\ncovered points is at most \\(2k+n\\).  Substituting \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) gives\n\\(2(n-1)+n=3n-2\\) and \\(2n+n=3n\\), respectively.  Both numbers are strictly\nsmaller than \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (the inequality\n\\(3n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) holds for \\(n\\ge4\\) and can be checked directly for\n\\(n=3\\)).  Therefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny, which yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 4.1.  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n-i-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so they belong to \\(S\\).  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(n-i-1)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently each \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are distinct (they have different\n\\(x\\)-intercepts) and each contains exactly the two points \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\).  \nThus the sunny lines cover  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\{P_i,Q_i\\},\\qquad |U|=2k .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2.  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT:=S\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\n*Observation.* In each column \\(x=j\\;(1\\le j\\le n)\\) at most one point of that\ncolumn lies in \\(U\\) (the point \\(P_j\\) when \\(j\\le k\\) or the point\n\\(Q_{j-1}\\) when \\(j-1\\le k\\)).  Hence every column still contains at least one\npoint of \\(T\\).\n\nWe now introduce exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\\)   (\\(n-k-1\\) lines);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)                 (1 line);\n* the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) (1 line).\n\nThe total number of non‑sunny lines is \\((n-k-1)+1+1=n-k\\), as required.\n\n#### 4.3.  Verification of coverage  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k-1\\).*  \nThe point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is among the chosen vertical\nlines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\ge n-k\\).*  \nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\); thus\n\\(b\\in\\{1,2\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n* If \\(b=2\\) then \\(a=n-1\\) (because \\(a+2\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the\n  only way to have \\(b=2\\) while remaining in \\(T\\) is \\(a=n-1\\); any smaller\n  \\(a\\) would give a point already covered by a sunny line).  \n  The point \\((n-1,2)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\), hence it lies on the line\n  \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nTogether with the sunny lines, all points of \\(S\\) are covered.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the required\nproperty holds.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 together with the counting argument forces \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\nsuch a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
