 lines must be non‑sunny**, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sufficiency** – for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we construct \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n-i-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Both points belong to \\(S\\) and the line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through them has slope \\(-2\\); therefore each\n   \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny. The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\) are distinct and\n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i\\cap S=\\{P_i,Q_i\\},\\qquad |\\,\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\sigma_i\\cap S\\,|=2k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Define the remaining set of points  \n\n   \\[\n   T:=S\\setminus\\bigl(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\sigma_i\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Observe that in each column \\(x=j\\;(1\\le j\\le n)\\) at most one point of that column lies in the\n   sunny part (namely \\(P_j\\) or \\(Q_{j-1}\\) when those indices are defined). Consequently every column\n   still contains at least one point of \\(T\\).\n\n   We now introduce exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n   * vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\\) (\\(n-k-1\\) lines);\n   * the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (1 line);\n   * the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to the forbidden diagonal) (1 line).\n\n   The total number of non‑sunny lines is \\((n-k-1)+1+1=n-k\\), as required.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(T\\) lies on one of these lines.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T\\).\n\n   - **Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-k-1\\).  \n     Then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is among the chosen vertical lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - **Case 2.** \\(a\\ge n-k\\).  \n     From the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\).\n\n       *Subcase 2.1.* \\(b=1\\).  \n       The point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n       *Subcase 2.2.* \\(b=2\\).  \n       Then \\(a=n-1\\) (otherwise \\((a,2)\\) would be one of the points \\(P_i\\) or \\(Q_i\\) that have been\n       removed). Hence \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by one of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   Together with (2) all points of \\(S\\) are covered.\n\n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the required covering property holds.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a\n   configuration exists.  \n\n   Hence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
