 1.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nA sunny line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) meets the sunny line in at most one point; therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of each row \\(y=b\\).  \nHence a sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny line.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\), then the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on it are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) of them.  \nIf the line is vertical, \\(x=a\\), then the admissible points are \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.  \nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\) and passes through the triangle, it must be of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nThe only such line that meets \\(\\mathcal P\\) in more than one point is \\(x+y=n+1\\), which contains precisely the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nThus any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound when a sunny line is present\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny.  \nLet \\(L_{s}\\) be a sunny line; it contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|\\le (n-1)+ (n-1)\\cdot n = n^{2}-1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Comparison with the required number of points\n\nFrom (4),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-1,\n\\]\n\nbecause\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\bigl(n^{2}-1\\bigr)\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2n^{2}+2}{2}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}+n+2}{2}\n= \