which contains precisely the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nThus any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound when a sunny line is present\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny.  \nLet \\(L_{s}\\) be a sunny line; it contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|\\le (n-1)+ (n-1)\\cdot n = n^{2}-1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Comparison with the required number of points\n\nFrom (4),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-1,\n\\]\n\nbecause\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\bigl(n^{2}-1\\bigr)\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-2n^{2}+2}{2}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}+n+2}{2}\n= \\frac{(n-2)(n+1)}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the inequality (5) is impossible; consequently **no configuration containing a sunny line can cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).**  \n\nHence every admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must consist solely of non‑sunny lines, and the number of sunny lines is forced to be\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\,y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct, each is either horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) or has slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)); therefore none of them is sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \n* If \\(b=n\\) then