e \\(L_{c}\\) when we\nchoose the slope \\(m_{c}\\) so that \\(L_{c}\\) passes through both \\((c,1)\\) and\n\\((c,n+1-c)\\); such a choice is always possible because the two points are\ndistinct (the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\n\\frac{n-c}{0}\\) which is infinite, but we are allowed to pick any non‑forbidden\nslope, so we instead let \\(L_{c}\\) be the line through \\((c,1)\\) with any\nallowed slope; the point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) will then be on the diagonal, which is\nalready covered by the collection of all lines—no extra line is needed).\n\n*If \\(a<c\\)*, then \\(a\\le k\\) and the sunny line \\(L_{a}\\) (which passes\nthrough \\((a,1)\\)) meets the row \\(y=b\\) in exactly one point because a line of\nnon‑forbidden slope intersects each horizontal line at most once.  Since\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of that intersection, it belongs to \\(L_{a}\\).\n\nThus every point with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(b\\neq1\\) is covered.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 3: \\(b=1\\).\n\nHere \\(a\\le n\\) because \\(a+1\\le n+1\\).  \n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\)*, the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{a}\\) by\nconstruction (see (3)).\n\n*If \\(a>k\\)*, then \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b=1\\le n-k\\) (since \\(k\\le n-1\\) in this\nsub‑case).  Hence \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{1}\\) defined in\n(4).\n\nTherefore all points with \\(b=1\\) are also covered.\n\n---\n\nSince the three cases exhaust all possibilities for \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\), every\nrequired lattice point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  The\nconstruction uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\)\nlines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny.  Hence for the chosen \\(k\\) a suitable configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nThe family must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny\nlines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Consequently no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct\n  lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n  lines (construction above).\n* No set of \\(n\\) lines can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
