recisely the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on the original diagonal \\(D_{m}\\) **and** are not already covered by the two axis‑parallel lines.  \n     Hence after the replacement, every point that was previously covered by \\(D_{m}\\) remains covered (either by \\(S_{m}\\) or by \\(L_{v}\\) or \\(L_{h}\\)).  \n\n     Performing this replacement for any \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(m\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n3. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   - Points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(L_{v}\\); points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(L_{h}\\).  \n   - For a point with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) let \\(m=a+b\\).  \n     *If the diagonal \\(D_{m}\\) was **not** replaced*, the point lies on \\(D_{m}\\).  \n     *If \\(D_{m}\\) was replaced by \\(S_{m}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=m\\) and both coordinates are at least \\(2\\); a direct substitution shows that such a point also satisfies the equation of \\(S_{m}\\):\n     \\[\n     b = \\frac{m-1}{m}a + 1 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad ma = (m-1)a + m \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+b=m .\n     \\]\n     Hence the point lies on \\(S_{m}\\).  \n   - Thus every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and the upper bound proved in step 1 shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non‑negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ satisfy the required condition.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\