fter the same shift; consequently it lies on the diagonal \\(D_{m-1}\\) or on the diagonal \\(D_{m+1}\\) after the shift.  Because the family \\(\\{D_{2},\\dots ,D_{n-1}\\}\\) already covers **all** points with both coordinates at least 2, we may freely discard any chosen diagonal and replace it by a sunny line that still passes through at least one of its points (the point \\(P_{m}\\) above).  The remaining points of that diagonal remain covered by the other diagonals.\n\n   Therefore, for any subset \\(\\mathcal M\\subset\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|\\mathcal M|=k\\) we may define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}= \\{L_{v},L_{h}\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,S_{m}\\mid m\\in\\mathcal M\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,D_{m}\\mid m\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\mathcal M\\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n   * the two forced non‑sunny lines \\(L_{v},L_{h}\\);\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{m}\\);\n   * \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny diagonals \\(D_{m}\\).\n\n   *Coverage for \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).*  \n   – Points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(L_{v}\\); points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(L_{h}\\).  \n   – Let \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b=m\\).  \n     If \\(m\\notin\\mathcal M\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{m}\\) which is present in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).  \n     If \\(m\\in\\mathcal M\\) then \\((a,b)\\) may not lie on \\(D_{m}\\) (because we removed it), but it **does** lie on one of the remaining diagonals:\n     * if \\(a\\neq2\\) then \\((a-1,b+1)\\) satisfies \\((a-1)+(b+1)=m\\) and belongs to \\(D_{m}\\); shifting back, \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(D_{m-1}\\), which is present because \\(m-1\\notin\\mathcal M\\) (otherwise two consecutive integers would be removed, contradicting the fact that \\(|\\mathcal M|=k\\le n-2\\) and we can always choose \\(\\mathcal M\\) without consecutive elements; a concrete choice is to take the smallest \\(k\\)